+## Conveyance efficiency and leakage
+
+For reach `r`, `eta[r] = edges.csv.utilization_ratio` when `0 < eta <= 1`; otherwise readable-LBBD retains its default `0.995`. The topology reader recognizes the exact header `utilization_ratio`; `seepageC` is not an alias. Configure this value on `indirect` reach rows.
+
+Both the Master and hydraulic SP apply efficiency to delayed incoming volume. The SP balance is structurally:
+
+`V[r,t+1] = V[r,t] + eta[r] * delayed_inflow[r,t] - turnout_delivery[r,t] - child_reach_inflow[r,t] - outlet[r,t] - spill[r,t] + side_inflow[r,t]`.
+
+Thus the physical reach loss is `(1-eta[r]) * raw_delayed_arrival`. Type-6 `leakage*` output gates expose that loss; multiple such gates on one reach split it evenly. Path efficiencies multiply, so a downstream demand can require progressively more upstream withdrawal. With demand held fixed, lowering `eta` should increase cumulative headworks/intermediate-reach flow until a source or reach capacity binds; terminal demand volume itself remains capped by demand. Compare `sum_t Q[t] dt[t]` rather than assuming every time-step value or the MIP-selected peak must increase.
+
