+# `edges.csv` topology and conveyance-loss validation
+
+Read this reference when validating topology capacity, conveyance efficiency, or leakage behavior in readable-LBBD.
+
+## Effective field
+
+`utilization_ratio` is the dimensionless fraction of delayed upstream flow that reaches the pool balance:
+
+```text
+effective arrival = utilization_ratio * raw delayed arrival
+reach loss        = (1 - utilization_ratio) * raw delayed arrival
+```
+
+- Configure it on `ConnectionType=indirect` rows, which define reaches/pools.
+- Use `(0,1]`: `1` means no loss, `0.995` means about 0.5% loss, and `0.95` means about 5% loss.
+- The C++ pool default and readable-LBBD reach default are both `0.995`.
+- The reader requires the exact header `utilization_ratio`. `seepageC` is not an alias and is ignored, so a file containing only `seepageC` behaves like an omitted field and retains `0.995`.
+- readable-LBBD accepts a loaded value only when `0 < value <= 1`; otherwise it uses `0.995`. Do not rely on that fallback because other solver paths may consume the pool value differently.
+
+The same reach efficiency is present in Exact Master balances, value/shaped hydraulic SP balances, path-capacity calculations, initial in-transit water, and validation. A loss test is therefore a model-input validation, not merely an output-format check.
+
+## Expected response
+
+With demand, capacities, initial state, and all other settings held fixed, lowering `utilization_ratio` normally causes:
+
+- larger integrated source/headworks flow;
+- larger integrated intermediate control-gate flow, with a stronger effect farther upstream or across more lossy reaches;
+- unchanged integrated terminal demand-gate delivery when the same demand remains fully satisfied;
+- larger sum of explicit `leakage*` outputs.
+
+Do not require every timestamp or peak to increase. A time-limited MIP may select a different feasible schedule, so peak and timing changes mix physical loss compensation with scheduling choices. Integrated volumes are the primary causal comparison. Capacity binding may instead reduce demand satisfaction.
+
+## Isolated A/B procedure
+
+1. Resolve the effective file through `OCIS_dataConfig.json`, `selected_model`, `dirpath`, `case_name`, and `mesh_path`. Do not assume `edges_path` is effective in the current MILP loader.
+2. Confirm the compiled test macro, case path, `stepSolver_MILP(...)` episode, executable hash, and process working directory.
+3. Copy the executable and one case into a new `.codex_validation/` directory. Run from the directory that makes the compiled relative DataConfig path valid.
+4. Preserve the CSV encoding. These case files may be GBK/GB18030; terminal mojibake is not evidence of corruption. Decode explicitly, edit only the exact header/value cells, re-encode, and verify node names and row count.
+5. Use one baseline and one changed efficiency. Do not change `input.json`, boundary demand, capacity, or topology in the same comparison.
+6. Require a usable detailed incumbent. Record exit code, wall time, `[LBBD][AllocationSummary]`, `[LBBD][Validation]`, automatic relaxations, and `optimality_proven`.
+7. From `output/Q.csv`, integrate with the modeled period length: `volume = sum_t Q[t] * period_seconds[t]`. Compare named headworks/control gates, terminal demand gates, and the sum of all `leakage*` columns. Do not use `d*` aggregate columns as substitutes for named gates.
+
+## Interpretation traps
+
+- Renaming `seepageC` to `utilization_ratio` while retaining value `1` changes the model from the implicit default `0.995` to zero loss. That is not a header-only equivalence test.
+- To prove a readable header is equivalent to the old implicit default, set every relevant `indirect` value explicitly to `0.995`; identical `Q.csv` numeric contents then establish equivalence for that run.
+- A 100% demand-satisfaction result does not mean losses are absent. Upstream withdrawal may have increased to preserve the same terminal delivery.
+- If the run did not prove optimality, report cumulative physical effects separately from peak/timing differences.
